2026/02/23 11:23:45 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 1: Proposed new text for self: text
Task: Extract “Unknown fact” statements from a clinical handover transcript.

Definition (extract ONLY when explicitly unknown):
An “Unknown fact” statement is an explicit declaration that the speaker does not know / is unsure about a specific fact (missing knowledge), e.g.:
- “I don’t know his allergies.”
- “Not sure if consent’s been signed.”
- “Unsure if he’s been up and about.”
- “I can’t recall …”
- “We don’t know …”
- “Unknown …”

Do NOT extract:
- Patient-limited phrasing like “No allergies that I know of.” (this is not the clinician stating a missing fact; treat as a normal patient report unless the speaker explicitly says the information is unknown/unavailable)
- Mere vagueness/hedging without explicit missing-knowledge (e.g., “at some point today”, “seems ok”, “reportedly”, “query”, “appears to be”)
- General uncertainty that is not clearly “we don’t know X” (if it’s not an explicit lack-of-knowledge statement, omit it)

Extraction rules:
1. Extract each unknown-fact statement as its own separate quote (one quote per statement).
2. Quotes must be copied EXACTLY from the transcript (verbatim), with the same punctuation/capitalization/typos; no paraphrasing or corrections.
3. Do not merge or stitch together text from different parts of the transcript (keep to the original contiguous wording).
4. If a single line contains an explicit unknown-fact statement plus extra unrelated content, quote only the minimal contiguous span that contains the explicit unknown-fact statement (but still exact text).
5. Label every extracted quote with label: "Unknown fact".
6. If there are no explicit unknown-fact statements, return an empty list [].

Output format:
Return a JSON list of objects, each like:
[
  {"label": "Unknown fact", "quote": "<exact quote>"}
]
If none: []
