2026/02/23 12:02:53 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 49: Proposed new text for self: text
Task: Extract “Unknown fact” statements from a clinical handover transcript.

Input:
- One field: `text` (a transcript; may contain speaker tags like [NURSE], [DOCTOR], [PATIENT], etc.).

Goal:
- Output ONLY clinician/speaker explicit statements of missing knowledge/uncertainty about a specific fact (i.e., the speaker directly indicates they do not know / are unsure / cannot recall whether something is true/available/completed/present).

What counts as an “Unknown fact” (must be explicit not-knowing):
Extract a quote ONLY if the transcript contains an explicit missing-knowledge marker tied to a concrete fact, e.g.:
- “I don’t know …”
- “We don’t know …”
- “I’m not sure if …”
- “Not sure whether …”
- “Unsure if/whether …”
- “I can’t recall …”
- “I don’t remember …”
- “Unknown …”
- “Not really sure …”
- “I have no idea …”
- “I haven’t been told … / I haven’t heard …” (ONLY if it clearly asserts lack of knowledge, not merely “no update yet”)

What does NOT count (do NOT extract):
1) Patient-limited / hearsay qualifiers that are not a clinician explicitly lacking knowledge:
   - Example: “None that I know of.” (treat as a normal report of allergies, NOT an unknown-fact statement for this task unless the clinician explicitly says they personally don’t know/are unsure)
2) Absence of an update is not automatically “unknown”:
   - Example: “The surgical team hasn’t provided an update yet on the timing” is NOT an Unknown fact unless paired with explicit uncertainty like “so I don’t know the timing yet” / “we’re not sure when it will be”
3) General hedging/vagueness without explicit not-knowing:
   - “seems…”, “appears…”, “probably…”, “reportedly…”, “query…”, “at some point…”
4) If there are no explicit unknown-fact statements → return []

Extraction / span-boundary rules (critical; span-based evaluation):
- Each unknown-fact statement must be a separate output object (do not combine multiple unknowns into one quote).
- Quotes must be VERBATIM substrings from the transcript (exact characters, capitalization, spacing, typos).
- Do not stitch non-contiguous text.
- If a line contains the unknown-fact statement plus other unrelated info, extract ONLY the minimal contiguous span that still clearly expresses explicit not-knowing.
- Boundary precision:
  - Prefer the shortest exact substring that contains the explicit unknown marker + the fact.
  - Do NOT include trailing punctuation (e.g., final period) unless it is required because it is part of the minimal span as written.
  - Do not include extra lead-in words if they aren’t needed to preserve the explicit “don’t know/unsure/can’t recall” meaning.

Output format:
- Return ONLY a JSON list of objects, exactly:
[
  {"label": "Unknown fact", "quote": "<exact quote>"}
]
- If none, return: []

Quality check before finalizing:
- For every candidate quote, confirm it includes an explicit not-knowing phrase (don’t know / not sure / unsure / can’t recall / unknown / etc.).
- If the text only indicates “no update yet” or “none that I know of” without clinician explicit uncertainty, exclude it.
